NAME
    menu - interactive terminal selector and filesystem browser

SYNOPSIS
    menu [-H header] [-F footer] [-B bottom-footer]
         [-K action-key ...] [-S toggle-key] [--] item ...

    menu [-H header] [-F footer] [-B bottom-footer]
         -K action-key [-K action-key ...] [-S toggle-key]
         [-n] [-m] [-c] -d directory

DESCRIPTION
    menu displays an interactive list on the terminal while reserving standard
    output for a machine-readable successful result.

    In list mode, items come from command-line operands. In filesystem mode,
    -d selects a starting directory and Enter browses subdirectories.

    Space toggles multi-selection by default. If no item is marked when an
    action finishes, the current item is returned. If items are marked, only
    the marked values are returned, in menu/provider order.

OPTIONS
    -H header
        Show header at the top of the menu. In filesystem mode the current
        absolute directory follows this text on the next line.

    -F footer
        Show footer immediately after the visible items.

    -B bottom-footer
        Anchor footer text at the bottom of the terminal.

    -K action-key
        Add an action key. May be repeated. List mode also always accepts Enter
        as confirmation. Filesystem mode requires at least one -K because Enter
        is reserved for browsing.

    -S toggle-key
        Change the multi-selection toggle key. The default is space. The
        configured toggle key is reserved and cannot also be an action key.

    -d directory
        Use filesystem mode rooted initially at directory.

    -n
        Filesystem mode only. Hide hidden entries. Hidden entries are shown by
        default.

    -m
        Filesystem mode only. Mix directories and non-directories in one
        lexical ordering. By default directories sort before other entries.

    -c
        Filesystem mode only. Confine browsing to the physically resolved
        subtree of the starting directory. The parent entry is omitted at the
        confinement root and directory symlinks cannot navigate outside it.

LIST ITEMS
    label
        Display and return the same string.

    value@:=label
        Display label and return value. The first @:= separates value from
        label.

KEYS
    Up, Down
        Move one item.

    PageUp, PageDown
        Move by one visible page.

    Home, End
        Move to the first or last item.

    Space
        Toggle the current mark by default.

    Enter
        In list mode, finish with action key enter. In filesystem mode, enter a
        selected directory; Enter on a non-directory does not finish.

    Escape
        Cancel.

    Configurable action/toggle keys may be one text key or these named keys:

        space nul tab backtab backspace left right insert delete f1 ... f20

    arrow_left and arrow_right are accepted command-line aliases for left and
    right. Navigation keys, Escape and Enter cannot be configured as -K/-S.

FILESYSTEM MODE
    The current directory is resolved physically and displayed in the header.
    Hidden entries are included unless -n is used. Directory groups and mixed
    listings use ascending bytewise lexical order.

    Returned values are absolute selected paths. Display sanitization used to
    keep unusual filenames on one terminal row does not replace the returned
    path value.

    Changing directory clears marks from the previous directory view.

OUTPUT
    On success, stdout contains one shell-safe serialized argument vector:

        action-key value-1 ... value-n

    The serialization is produced with the m quote primitive. To consume it:

        result=$(menu ...)
        status=$?
        [ "$status" -eq 0 ] || exit "$status"
        eval "set -- $result"
        key=$1
        shift

    "$@" then contains the selected values exactly as shell strings. Do not use
    unquoted word splitting such as set -- $(menu ...).

    The same result can initialize an array after successful execution:

        eval "array result set $result"

    Canonical result key names include enter, space, tab, left, right and the
    configured text/function-key token.

RETURN STATUS
    0   Successful action result.
    1   User/provider cancellation.
    2   Invalid invocation or menu/provider/terminal failure.

    Signal-derived statuses from an interrupted interactive session are
    propagated.

